s. So $0086 = Mario's world/screen x (page 0). Writing it moved Mario. \n\nBut wait — is the red thing Mario or the goomba? The goomba is brown with black eyes, distinctly not red. This blob has red. Let me zoom by taking get_pixel_color or list_sprites. Let me use list_sprites to find Mario.\n\nActually, let me be rigorous: use list_sprites to see sprites. Or get_tile_at_pixel at (200,196).\n\nLet me call list_sprites and get_pixel_color.\n\nActually, simplest: screenshot shows Mario at ~x=200. Let me confirm with list_sprites.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: I wrote $0086=200 while the game was unpaused (running). The write is live, so the game would process it next frame. It seems to have worked: Mario jumped right.\n\nBut hold on, if $0086 were merely a derived copy (recomputed each frame from Mario's real position), then writing it wouldn't move Mario; instead Mario would stay and $0086 would snap back to 40. Unless the game reads $0086 as the source. The screenshot suggests Mario moved. Let me verify with list_sprites and reading $0086 back.\n\nLet me do list_sprites and read $0086, $0005, $073F."}
